date-released: "2026-11-16"
keywords:
  - misinformation detection
  - fact-checking
  - retrieval-augmented generation
  - COVID-19 vaccines
  - YouTube
  - speech transcription
abstract: >-
  The spread of misinformation on video platforms such as YouTube, where health claims reach large audiences while personalized recommendations shield them from correction, calls for verification that is both automated and grounded in evidence. This work proposes a complete methodology for detecting misinformation in YouTube videos using large language models (LLMs) without task-specific training. Each video is transcribed automatically, segmented, and judged by a language model: once from the model's parametric knowledge alone, and once via Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) against passages retrieved from a curated corpus of authoritative and peer-reviewed sources, supplemented by an adaptive web search mechanism. Because the reference corpus is a modular, replaceable component, the methodology is topic-agnostic. We instantiate the pipeline on COVID-19 vaccine claims and evaluate it across four open-weight models from three developers on a test set of 119 manually labeled videos. All four models identified nearly every video containing fake news from their own knowledge, with false positives arising mostly in ambiguous cases. Using retrieved evidence to verify these predictions reduced false alarms and improved the F1-score of every model. These findings indicate that a curated body of evidence is most effective as a verifier of parametric judgments in automated fake news detection.
